{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 26, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "the social network (2010) is a razor-sharp, dialogue-driven drama that dissects the birth of facebook and the messy human fallout that followed. directed by david fincher with a script by aaron sorkin, the film is a masterclass in pacing, wit, and moral ambiguity, wrapped in a sleek, modern aesthetic that feels both timeless and distinctly contemporary.\n\nthe story unfolds through a series of flashbacks as mark zuckerberg (played with icy precision by jesse eisenberg) is sued by former classmates edwardo severin (andrew Garfield) and the winklevoss twins (armie hammer in a dual role) for stealing their idea. simultaneously, zuckerberg faces a lawsuit from his former best friend and business partner, eduardo savarin (brendan fraser), played with heartbreaking vulnerability by andrew garfield. the film navigates these legal battles while also exploring the personal relationships that unravel in the wake of facebook’s explosive success.\n\njesse eisenberg’s performance as zuckerberg is nothing short of electrifying. he captures the character’s genius, arrogance, and emotional detachment with unsettling accuracy. zuckerberg isn’t just a villain or a hero—he’s a deeply flawed human being whose ambition borders on sociopathy, yet his flashes of insecurity make him oddly sympathetic. the supporting cast is equally stellar: armie hammer’s portrayal of the twins is both hilarious and tragic, playing up their privileged obliviousness while hinting at their simmering resentment. andrew garfield’s eduardo is the emotional core of the film, embodying the betrayed idealist whose loyalty is crushed under zuckerberg’s ruthless drive.\n\nthe dialogue is sorkin at his finest—fast, dense, and dripping with sarcasm and subtext. the film’s script crackles with energy, making even the most mundane legal proceedings feel like a high-stakes game of chess. the soundtrack, composed by trent reznor and atticus ross, is a haunting blend of electronic and orchestral elements that perfectly complements the film’s themes of isolation, ambition, and the cold machinery of capitalism.\n\nvisually, fincher’s direction is immaculate. the use of lighting and framing often feels clinical, reinforcing zuckerberg’s emotional distance, while the editing (by kirk baxter and angus wall) keeps the film brisk and engaging. the film’s depiction of harvard’s ivy-covered elitism contrasts sharply with the sterile, blue-tinted world of silicon valley, highlighting how reputation and money corrupt even the most revolutionary ideas.\n\nthe social network isn’t just a movie about facebook—it’s a meditation on innovation, betrayal, and the cost of success. it asks whether genius excuses ruthlessness and whether an idea can ever belong to one person. the film’s ending is deliberately ambiguous, leaving us to wonder if zuckerberg is a visionary or just a man who stumbled into history while burning every bridge behind him.\n\nin short, the social network is a modern classic—smart, biting, and endlessly quotable. it’s a film that demands to be watched multiple times, each viewing revealing new layers in its razor-sharp script and performances. if you love films that are as intellectually stimulating as they are entertaining, this is a must-watch. 10 out of 10.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.3156192898750305}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.003911227453500032}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " (", "logprob": -1.078153371810913}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.0003854485403280705}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -2.861018856492592e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.1920922133867862e-06}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -3.2186455882765586e-06}, {"token": ")", "logprob": -0.348632276058197}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.09846502542495728}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.014774550683796406}, {"token": " razor", "logpr